Suppose that for every element $i$ of a directed set $(I, \leq)$ we have a set $A_{i}$, and for every inequality $i \leq j$ we have a map $\alpha_{j i}: A_{i} \rightarrow A_{j}$. If

\begin{itemize}
  \item[(a)] $\alpha_{i i}=\mathrm{id}$ for all $i \in I$, and
  \item[(b)] $\alpha_{k j} \circ \alpha_{j i}=\alpha_{k i}$ whenever $i \leq j \leq k$,
\end{itemize}

then the family $\left(A_{i}, \alpha_{j i}\right)$ is called a direct system.

There is for each $i$ a canonical map

